it's great to welcome to the program  today Roy Germano who is an author  documentary filmmaker and NYU professor  Roy has some great videos on his YouTube  channel where he is hanging out with a  rabbi a very charismatic rabbi in the  Crown Heights Brooklyn neighborhood and  so talk to us about that Roy I mean how  did how did that idea start of going you  actually go to one night of Hanukkah  with a family in Crown Heights how did  this all start it started with the tour  that the rabbi gives so I live in Crown  Heights I live about two subway stops  from where he lives and I just noticed  that he was giving these tours of his  neighborhood and I had always been  curious about Hasidic Jews having lived  in Brooklyn for ten years and also  feeling like people like me and people  like him don't usually talk so I took my  stepdaughter on the tour we we loved it  and through that Rabbi Katz and I  started hanging out like we would just  get coffee and talk about meaning of  life and stuff like that so I'm not  Jewish I'm not religious so it was just  it was just a great experience to talk  to somebody that I normally wouldn't be  talking to like that so one day we got  the idea to make a video of us hanging  out and it did pretty well on YouTube so  we decided to make more yeah and one of  the things I've talked about for years  is the the kind of mixed feeling I have  when you know I hear that a religious  conservative from Iowa who has been  anti-gay of politically for 20 years of  has finds out that they have a child who  is LGBT and then the view radically  changes because I know it touches them  personally and on the one hand it's  great that they are not so far gone that  a personal connection can't change their  mind but at the same time I wish it  didn't require that to be able to put  ourselves in the shoes of others and  imagine what it would be like so that  I'm curious to start with did your  experience hanging out with the Hasidic  Jewish community in Brooklyn did it  change your perspective in some sense on  any broader social or political issue  that might  late to that community yeah I mean what  you're describing is rooted in a social  psychology research there was a great  book called the nature of prejudice by  Gordon outport from the 50s and it's  really about this idea that it's hard to  hate somebody that you get to know  that's sort of my paraphrase of of  Albert's theory and I think it's true I  mean a lot of us have difficulty  imagining what life is like for others  so to spend time with them at the end of  the day we end up seeing that they're  not just a member of a group but they're  a human being and they're an individual  and they have you know their own  differences with that group and their  own similarities and you you can choose  to dislike them or like them on the  basis of them as an individual not as a  member of a group for me personally I  think by hanging out in the historic  neighborhood here in Crown Heights I've  just learned that the people who live  there are normal people I guess for me  and for a lot of people here in Brooklyn  you pass Hasidic people all the time and  you just assume that perhaps they think  and talk completely differently and then  when you sit down and you you realize  that you know we're all just sort of  trying to figure things out on our own  and they do it their way we do it our  way so I'd say that's been the number  one thing is just realizing that they're  like regular people just like you and me  a lot of the realizing that they're  regular people thing in terms of  American politics plays a huge role in  the immigration debate that that is now  being had you've spent time in Mexico  you talk about immigration a lot what is  the what are the ways in which the  experience with the Hasidic community in  Crown Heights has sort of informed or  made you think about what is happening  in terms of the immigration debate right  now particularly from Mexico and points  further south and central in Latin  America it's almost the reverse it's my  experience with Mexican and Central  American immigrants that that is in  foreign my approach to the hate crime  and an issue here in Crown Heights so  about 1560 however many years ago I was  a waiter in Chicago and I worked with a  lot of undocumented immigrants from  Mexico at that restaurant and to that  point in my life I had never met anybody  from Mexico and I never  at an undocumented person and so it was  like this process of realizing hey these  guys that I'm working with are just  normal people they're not the kind of  you know drug cartel  you know henchmen that you see and in  movies they didn't fit this stereotype  of Mexico that I had seen in the movies  throughout my life they were funny they  were kind they were intelligent they  were impressive people so it was through  that process of working at the  restaurant that I started to see the  humanity of immigrants and and just  really spent a big part of my career  going to Mexico learning about the  immigration issue and and then  eventually making films about it to kind  of bring that information back here to  the United States so this Crown Heights  project is really just an extension of a  project that I started in Mexico fifteen  years ago when I look at the  conversations that happen about  immigration in politics whether you're  looking at left or right there are some  themes that always come up what it what  is the right number of people that  should be allowed in what are we allowed  to do what should we be doing at the  border in terms of protection how much  of a focus should overstayed visas be  which source countries should we give  preference to in terms of allowing  people and for me the the big thing  that's missing is the discussion of what  is it that is generating the desire to  come to the United States in different  countries and it seems to me that  actually putting resources into dealing  with that may do way more than any of  the other piecemeal things that are  being heavily debated what's your sense  of that and what what are the broader  broader sort of economic questions  around the issue of immigration exactly  I think you've hit the nail on the head  there that when I would go to Mexican  communities back when I was doing my PhD  research I would hear the same story  over and over it was usually something  like we used to grow X crop then  suddenly almost overnight the price of  that crop fell in half and then people  here could no longer make a living and  so that that price drop that people were  talking about was usually related to  larger changes in the global economy  sort of the neoliberal ization of the  global economy the loosening of  regulations the cutting of social  welfare welfare program  and literally that the first community  ever went to in Mexico was a coffee  growing community and people would talk  about how the price dropped overnight  and when I looked at the larger changes  that were happening I saw that that had  a lot to do with US foreign economic  policy and then later I would talk to  corn farmers and they would talk about  the price of corn dropping and I would  see that that would have a lot to do  with NAFTA and the export of US corn to  Mexico and importation into Mexico  so I started to get the sense that if  you even tweak small things in our in  our foreign economic policy with  countries like Mexico and Central  America you could see a vast reduction  in the number of people who are  migrating without documentation and then  the Central American case is is slightly  different because there's the violence  issue there but you can even look at how  our environmental policies contribute to  climate change which has contributed to  massive droughts in Central America that  if in turn made it harder for people to  make a living which then has resulted in  more people coming to the US so  everything's interconnected in that  sense I want to try to make an analogy  between immigration in the United States  - trade policy as it relates to China so  we now are dealing with the the trade  war that is being waged against China  and we hear about oh we have a trade  deficit and Donald Trump wants to fix  the trade deficit and it's a very micro  focus and if you step back you realize  that for decades we've had an economy  based on we want cheap stuff  manufactured overseas that that's sort  of the basis and that's a lot of the  reason why we have a trade deficit today  and if we fix the trade deficit all of  these other things will have to change  it won't necessarily be good or bad but  it will have to change and it's not  being accounted for in the same way the  analogy I would make to immigration in  the United States is that a lot of those  people that I see arguing for the for  the sort of strict right-wing policies  that that we've been seeing don't seem  to account for the reality of industries  that are heavily reliant on both the  documented and undocumented immigrant  labor and even if we take them at their  face that these are the lists of things  that they want to fix there seems to be  very little consideration  for the broader economic impact that  that would have and it's not you know  you start to get a little bit I don't  want to start saying look at the  restaurant industry and agriculture and  and sort of and and creating these  stereotypes necessarily but there's a  reality that certain industries would be  very different were it not for the  status quo that exists today how should  we be thinking about that at the  individual level and how should  policymakers think about that yeah this  is actually an area where I think that  there could be a lot of common ground  between Democrats and Republicans if you  do look at the ways in which some of our  communities depend upon immigrants I  made a documentary a few years ago when  I was working with Vice news about the  dairy industry in upstate New York and  that there's so many people actually  that are very politically conservative  their farm owners but they've come to  appreciate the contributions that  immigrants make to their community  because they know that they can't find  people to do entry-level dairy farming  work from the the local community but  there are so many people that actually  have the skills that are coming from  agricultural communities in Central  America and Mexico that are interested  in doing that work and it's just amazing  it goes back to what I said at the  beginning that it's hard to hate  somebody that you get to know you see  these politically conservative farmers  becoming  over the years very close with the  people that work on their farms I met  farmers who actually travel to Mexico to  go to weddings and and meet the extended  families of the people that have worked  on their farms for you know 10 or 20  years so in some sense I think that you  know you have that economic reality and  and we could come together on that but  also a process of getting to know each  other I think could could contribute to  to policy outcomes that lead to more  compassionate stance on immigration what  do you think the priorities should be in  terms of what the US can do and that  could be what we can do within our  borders but also we have the decisions  that we can make about how we want to  work with other countries that are  sources of immigration to the United  States is there lower the lowest hanging  fruit that you see that are the these  things should be done right away  you know it is tricky in terms of  working for example with certain Central  American countries I mean there's a huge  culture of corruption in a country like  Honduras for example where I did some  work but there are a number of  organizations down there that are  working to reduce gang violence so  working to bolster those organizations  and invest in those organizations there  are various educational organizations  like for example there's a organization  that I'm familiar with called Becca  which is a an organization in Honduras  that provides bilingual education to  young people and they've had such a  great record where none of their  students have migrated illegally to the  United States they found work in Central  America so I think working with those  organizations would be a great great  first step when it comes to the economic  arguments about immigrants are sending  money back to their home countries and  this is a drain on the United States I  sort of have two questions on that one  is is it true that it is a drain and do  we not have to account for the other  side of the equation which is that  particularly undocumented immigrants are  often paying into social welfare systems  that because they are undocumented they  will never take benefits out and are  actually bolstering some of those  systems economically yeah so I wrote a  book called outsourcing welfare it's all  about the money that immigrants sent to  their home countries and this really is  you know a massive source of aid to  developing countries throughout the  world not just Mexico and Central  America but throughout the world it's  worth three times more than the aid that  the world's richest countries donate to  the developing world so it's just a huge  huge source of income for for low and  middle-income countries I do get this  question a lot is it a drain on the  United States to see this money go out  you know compared to the GDP and the  size of the United States economy it's  really a drop in the bucket the amount  of money going out this money does  create jobs actually there's so many  startups in the FinTech world and of  course you have like Western Union and  MoneyGram and those businesses that make  a lot of money off the the transfer of  migrant money I do think they're fiche  be reduced yes at this just my  perspective on that and and then a lot  of times people are buying American  products even in in migrants homelands  with with this money so it's not like  the money completely goes away in terms  of the money that immigrants pay into  the system I actually interviewed the  chief statistician at the Social  Security Administration a few years ago  and he estimates that undocumented  immigrants pay about thirteen billion  dollars into the Social Security system  and they'll never see that money back  and the way that that happens is that a  lot of undocumented immigrants get jobs  using fake Social Security numbers and  then they're on normal payrolls and  they're getting paid a paycheck and out  of that money comes the payroll taxes  which go to the to the federal  government so and you know undocumented  immigrants pay all kinds of taxes as  well they pay you know sales taxes and  property taxes and in all that as well  so it's not like they're just living off  of the system as as many people would  want to believe last thing I want to  touch on whenever it is that the current  president leaves office whether it's in  January of 2021 or January of 2025 or  sometime in between or before at this  point it's anyone's guess when it's  going to happen what are the things that  most immediately should be reversed that  he has done when it comes to immigration  well I mean his his effect on the  immigration system is not just what he's  done in terms of hard policy but it's  kind of the chaos that he's created in  the system for example I have friends  and colleagues that work in refugee  resettlement and for them it's just sort  of an open question of what the heck is  gonna happen right nobody really knows  how many refugees are gonna be able to  resettle so he's managed to just really  gum up the works and create all kinds of  uncertainty so there's that I think the  wall for example it's just ridiculous  and I've been saying this for years I  made a video in 2010 that you can find  on YouTube of two Americans climbing the  wall in less than 18 seconds and I've  always called the wall a symbol in the  desert it's just this very expensive  symbol that some politicians want to  build so that they say that they're  doing something about the issue you  could take all of those bill  of dollars and invest that in  communities in a place like Honduras and  and see huge huge return on that  investment in terms of people's lives  being improved and then and fewer people  coming to the United States we've been  speaking with NYU professor Royce Romano  who's an author documentary filmmaker  you can check out some of his work by if  you are watching on YouTube checking out  the links down in the video description  roy such a pleasure having you on I  really appreciate your time thanks David  thanks so much

